<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Player, skill, camera, arena, growth and coffee. One file because tuning any of them means looking
  at the others: raising moveSpeed without checking the enemy table is how a build stops being playable.
-->
<Config>

  <!--
    maxSan 99 rather than 100. Every ceiling in this game is 99, and a player who notices it once
    starts looking for it everywhere.

    moveSpeed 4.5 is the number every enemy speed was chosen against. Raising it makes the whole
    enemy table trivially outrunnable, lowering it makes Deadline at 4.0 undodgeable. Treat it as
    fixed and tune the enemies instead.

    def 0 and dodge 0 at the start on purpose: the first armour piece has to feel like it did something,
    and a player who starts at 30 percent reduction cannot feel the difference of the next 10.
  -->
  <Player maxSan="99" atk="10" critChance="5" critMulti="200"
          def="0" dodge="0" dodgeCap="60"
          moveSpeed="4.5" haste="0" luck="0"
          invulnAfterHit="0.6"
          pickupRadius="1.6" stepPickupRadius="0.6"
          radius="0.4" />

  <!--
    摸鱼. Heals only 5 percent, so it is an escape button and not a heal button. The push exists to
    break a surround; without it the invulnerability just delays the same death by 1.5 seconds.

    pushForce is a distance in units, same as every knockback in Weapons.xml. It is the one push in
    the game allowed to be this large, because breaking a surround is the entire reason it exists.
  -->
  <Skill id="slack" name="摸鱼" cd="12" invulnDuration="1.5" healPctMaxSan="5"
         pushRadius="3" pushForce="4" />

  <!--
    orthographicSize 5.5 at 16:9 gives a 19.6 x 11 view, so the camera half extents are 9.78 x 5.5.

    Those two numbers decide the spawn band, and the relationship is not the obvious one. An ellipse
    only encloses the screen rectangle when (halfW/semiX)^2 + (halfH/semiY)^2 <= 1: matching the axes
    to the screen leaves the ellipse cutting through all four corners, and enemies pop in diagonally.
    The band in Days.xml is sized against that inequality, so changing this line means resizing it too.
  -->
  <Camera orthographicSize="5.5" followLerp="6" aspect="1.7777778" />

  <!--
    Bounded field. A bounded arena is what stops "run left forever" from being the correct answer to
    pressure, and it is the precondition for the spawn band's edge fallback rule.

    Sized off the band, not off the screen: the band is anchored on the player, so the arena has to
    contain semi axis + outward push + edge margin or the top and bottom sectors fail their placement
    check on every attempt and the directional weighting quietly stops working.
  -->
  <!-- 19.71 / 11.5 matches the full 1834 x 1070 office map without cropping or stretching. -->
  <Arena halfWidth="19.71" halfHeight="11.5" />

  <!--
    ceil(coef * L^power) per level, 9 levels for the 9 job ranks.

    The rank curve is the anchor, not the two numbers, because it drives equipment card quality, the
    number of card choices and the result screen copy all at once. The sheet's stated intent is
    「玩家正好在周六升到 CEO」, so the cumulative cost to rank 9 is fitted just above what the first five
    days can supply: promoted to CEO, then immediately the annual review.

    The exp supply is whatever the spawn table pays out, so the curve has to be refitted every time
    that table is rescaled. It has been rescaled twice. Doubling it took the supply from roughly 1230
    exp across six days to roughly 2480 and power went 1.55 -> 1.95 to match; pulling a quarter back
    off leaves roughly 1860 and power comes down to 1.80. Miss either refit and the player is CEO on
    Wednesday with the back half of the week holding no level ups at all, which is the exact failure
    the 10 -> 12 change was made to avoid.

    power carries the change rather than coef, and which one moves is not arbitrary. Level 1 costs
    coef x 1, so the coefficient is also the price of the first upgrade, and that upgrade is the one
    number in this file that is a promise to the player rather than a tuning knob: it has to land
    inside ten seconds. Moving coef would move it off six mails. Leaving it alone pins level 1 at 12
    exp no matter what power does, and puts the whole of the adjustment on levels 5 through 9, which
    is also where the difference in supply actually shows up. Cumulative cost to rank 9 tracks the
    supply: 1103 at 1.55, about 2266 at 1.95, about 1711 at 1.80.

    Both this and kpiTargetKills below are fitted against an estimated supply rather than a measured
    one. Run the headless self test and correct them from its output: 'last rank on day' has to read
    6, and if it reads 4 or 5 the supply was underestimated and power goes up, never coef.

    downgradeExp 3: a drop that cannot beat what is worn is still worth walking over. Without it the
    late game turns the floor into scenery, which quietly deletes the reason to move.

    kpiTargetKills 840 against the roughly 900 bodies the week is now expected to produce, so the bar
    reaches the 99 cap partway through Saturday and clips. It has to clip somewhere: a target the run
    cannot reach leaves the bar at 98 and the cap never reads as a cap, while a much lower target
    parks it at 99 from Thursday onward and the cap stops reading as a cap and starts reading as a
    full bar. This one is pure bookkeeping against the spawn table, so it moves by the same proportion
    every time that table does. Set it to about 94 percent of the self test's 'total kills'.
  -->
  <Progression maxLevel="9" expCoef="12" expPower="1.80" downgradeExp="3"
               kpiCap="99" kpiTargetKills="840" finalSalary="9996" />

  <!--
    The drop rate doubles below one third sanity. This is the one number in the game that lies to the
    player in their favour, and it is invisible by design: felt as a lucky break, never as a handout.
  -->
  <Coffee chancePct="4" lowSanChancePct="8" lowSanThresholdPct="33"
          healPctMaxSan="12" hasteAddPct="15" buffSeconds="4" viewId="v_coffee" />

</Config>
